🚽 Test case: regression -- evaluate_expression_int()'s NODE_UNARY_OPERATION/
🚽 OP_DEREFERENCE case did `*(int *)pointee` unconditionally, correct only
🚽 when the pointee is a scalar variable's own 4-byte union slot, not
🚽 when it's a packed 1-byte array/struct slot (the same class of bug
🚽 fixed for NODE_ARRAY_ACCESS in the same PR).
🚽
🚽 `*p` alone in a native-call argument (e.g. `yapping("%d", *p)`) does
🚽 NOT exercise this function -- native-argument marshalling has its own,
🚽 separate (already-correct) path. Forcing arithmetic context with
🚽 `*p + 0` is what actually routes through evaluate_expression_int()'s
🚽 OP_DEREFERENCE case. Without the fix, reading `*p` at `a[0]` pulled in
🚽 `a[1]`/`a[2]`/`a[3]`'s real bytes as the upper 3 bytes of a bogus int
🚽 (confirmed: 2054842738, not 114) -- deterministic garbage, not
🚽 allocator-slack luck, since the neighboring bytes are real non-zero
🚽 data here rather than padding.
skibidi main {
    yap a[4];
    a[0] = 'r';
    a[1] = 'i';
    a[2] = 'z';
    a[3] = 'z';

    yap *p = &a[0];
    yapping("%d", *p + 0);
}
